One-Way, Two-Way & Three-Way Tables
Learn to organize data, step by step

Dataset: Survey of 100 Students (Course, Internship, Gender)

1) One-Way Table
Definition: A One-Way Table shows the frequency (count) of just one variable. It is also called a Frequency Table.

Course| Students| Percent
 BCA  |   40    | 40%
 BBA  |   35    | 35%
 BCom |   25    | 25%
Total |   100   | 100%

Insight: Most students are from BCA (40%).
When to Use: When you want to find the count or percentage of a single category.


2) Two-Way Table
Definition: A Two-Way Table shows two variables together. It is also called Cross-Tabulation.

Variables:
- Course
- Internship

Course| Internship: Yes| Internship: No| Total
 BCA  |      28        |       12      | 40
 BBA  |      14        |       21      | 35
 BCom |      8         |       17      | 25
 Total|     50         |       50      | 100

Insight: 70% of BCA students (28/40) completed an internship, compared to only 32% of BCom students (8/25).
When to Use: When you want to understand the relationship between two variables.


3) Three-Way Table
Definition: A Three-Way Table shows three variables together. Here, Course and Internship are analyzed based on Gender.

Variables:
- Course
- Internship
- Gender

Course| Male Yes| Male No| Female Yes| Female No| Total
  BCA |    18   |   6    |     10    |     6    |  40
  BBA |    7    |   11   |     7     |     10   |  35
  BCom|    4    |   9    |     4     |     8    |  25
 Total|    29   |   26   |     21    |     24   |  100

Insight: BCA has the highest internship rate (Male: 75%, Female: 62.5%). BCom has a comparatively low internship rate for both genders.


 Quick Summary

Table    | Variables | Purpose
One-Way  |     1     | Frequency / Percentage
Two-Way  |     2     | Find Relationships
Three-Way|     3     | Deeper Insights

Key Takeaway: The more relevant variables you add, the deeper the insights you can discover. 
However, larger tables can be harder to understand, so include only the variables that matter.
